\caption{Fig. 3: Pre-processing for frequency synchronized signals}
\end{figure}

The main challenge is that data must be collected OTA so as to capture realistic channel conditions and prepare the Artificial Intelligence (AI) to operate correctly in a wireless deployment with real radios. Additionally, nodes performing spectrum sensing might not always be aware of where signals are being transmitted, and their center frequency will rarely match that of the signals the sensing node is trying to detect, classify and locate. In Section V-C, we demonstrate how training on data that always assumes synchronization between sensing and transmission center frequencies results in poor accuracy.

Undoubtedly, one could perform a large OTA data collection campaign. However, such a task would be extremely hard and time-consuming due to (i) the several many possible combinations to be covered in time and frequency, which exponentially grow in the case of wideband spectrum sensing; and (ii) the need for large portions of spectrum without any interference from external systems. Our goal is to mitigate the complexity via a combination of OTA data collection and data augmentation.

Approach in a nutshell. First, we create a signal bank (Section III-A), i.e., a dataset containing individual signals collected OTA in the absence of interference. This serves as a seed to create much larger and more diverse datasets. Then, our generator (Section III-B) extracts multiple signals from the signal bank and combines them together (via time and frequency shift mechanisms) to create a semi-augmented "stitched signal" portraying a more realistic OTA data capture. These signals will interfere with each other, be in randomized frequency locations, and will have already experienced the common channel effects (fading, multipath, etc.) during OTA collection.

\section*{A. Signal Bank Generation}

The signal bank consists of a labeled collection of individual signals collected OTA when (i) only one signal is transmitted at a time; (ii) the center frequency and bandwidth are known apriori; and (iii) data collection is performed over a limited and small portion of the spectrum that is constantly monitored to ensure the lack of interference from external systems.

To be added to the signal bank, signals first go through a pre-processing pipeline shown in Fig. 3. Specifically, signals are broken up into shorter signals that are (i) cropped to contain only the actual signal transmission (e.g., by removing the silence period before and after data transmission); and (ii) bandpass-filtered to only extract the signal of interest and re-

