\section*{B. Classification Performance}

To evaluate the performance of our semantic segmentation model, we compare it with the original U-Net [25]. The mean IoU are \(96.70 \%, 93.44 \%\) for ours and U-Net respectively. Figure 8 demonstrates the IoU performance for each wireless technology, in which ours can achieve \(97.08 \%, 93.29 \%\), \(98.72 \%, 99.65 \%\) and \(94.74 \%\) for WiFi, LTE, Zigbee, LoRa and BLE signals. On the other hand, the U-Net has \(90.31 \%\), \(85.20 \%, 98.40 \%, 99.59 \%\) and \(93.72 \%\) IoU for five technologies respectively. Although our model is \(3 \%\) better than U-Net in general, it can achieve \(7 \%\) accuracy better than U-Net on signals with larger bandwidth such as WiFi and LTE due to the nonlocal block that we have introduced exactly to improve the detection of signals that occupy large portions of spectrum.

As mentioned in Section IV-A, our semantic spectrum segmentation classifier outputs a binary array of size \(C \times n_{i q}\) to determine which type of signal is present in each I/Q sample. In Fig. 9, we show two examples of inputs and outputs to illustrate the ability of our solution in detecting and individually classifying with high-resolution overlapping signals of different technologies. We show the class-specific output for the classes related to the overlapping signals only. Fig. 9 (left) shows how the classifier is able to effectively classify simultaneously overlapping WiFi and Zigbee signals, while Fig. 9 (right) shows the same but for LTE and BLE overlapping signals.

